            <div xml:id="d352">

                <pb facs="0355" n="347" xml:id="pg347"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.24/551: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-05T08:00:00+01:00">March 5, 1938—8
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>91. In the <hi rendition="#italic">Angriff</hi>, the press organ of Minister
                    Goebbels, an alleged request of the Department to the “Red Spanish Embassy” to
                    desist from armament purchases in America was criticised as being merely
                    intended to “save face”. In a short editorial yesterday which concluded “that
                    one will nevertheless continue to make a profit—industry will simply negotiate
                    direct—is a neat proof of the skill of the Foreign Office of the United States.
                    The arms dealers will well know how to appreciate such skill.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
